    "lawCode": "Amd §2805-i, Pub Health L; amd §631, Exec L; amd §§3216, 3221 & 4303, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6964A\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, the executive law and the insur-\nance law, in relation to sexual assault forensic exams\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo clarify sexual assault forensic exams performed by hospitals are free\nof charge to the sexual assault survivor.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends Public Health Law § 2805-i to clarify and ensure\nhospitals are aware of provisions of the Executive Law that prohibit\nhospitals from billing sexual assault survivors for the performance of\nsexual assault forensic exams. Further, this section clarifies that\nhospitals must notify orally and in writing to the sexual assault survi-\nvor of the option to decline to provide private health insurance infor-\nmation and to have the Office of Victim Services reimburse the hospital\nfor the exam.\n\nSection two amends Executive Law § 631 to provide, in instances where\nthe sexual assault survivor does assign private health insurance to\ncover the cost of the exam, they shall not be billed for any cost shar-\ning or otherwise balance billed.\n\nSection three, four and five amend Insurance Law §§ 3216(i), 3221(1) and\n4303(rr) to prohibit the imposition of any cost sharing for health care\nforensic examinations.\n\nSection six provides that the act shall take effect immediately and\napply to all policies and contracts issues, renewed or amended on or\nafter January 1st next succeeding enactment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2003 the state enacted the sexual assault examiner program. Under\nthis program, which is codified in Executive Law § 631(13), any state\naccredited hospital, sexual assault examiner program, or licensed health\ncare provider furnishing services to any sexual assault survivor,\nincluding a forensic examination in accordance with sex offense evidence\ncollection, shall provide the services without charge and shall bill the\nOffice of Victim Services (OVS) directly.  The OVS currently reimburses\nthe hospital $800 (or an amount adjusted by OVS, in collaboration with\nthe Department of Health annually) and the hospital or other provider\nmust accept this fee as payment in full. The law further provides that a\nhospital may request an assignment of private health insurance benefits\nonly when the sexual assault survivor has been advised orally and in\nwriting that he or she may decline to provide such information and the\nexam fee will be paid by OVS.\n\nRecent reports have suggested that some hospitals are unaware of these\nprotections and how billing should be properly processed for the\nperformance of sexual assault forensic exams. After receiving\ncomplaints, an investigation by the Attorney General alleges that one\nparticular hospital was repeatedly billing patients directly in\nviolation of current Executive Law provisions. By cross-referencing\nthese protections in the Public Health Law, under current hospital\nrelated provisions, this legislation will raise awareness and better\nensure adherence. Furthermore, by prohibiting insurance cost sharing or\nother balance billing, this bill will ensure a victim is never billed\nfor a forensic exam.\n\nNew York has made great strides in advancing sexual assault survivor\nrights, including recently enacted provisions requiring the timely proc-\nessing of all rape kits. As we move forward with such advancement, it is\nimperative that these statutory protections are being adhered to.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
